it was on monday that eunice first  became a named storm and by the time she  arrived she'd more than lived up to it  approaching the uk the storm underwent  what meteorologists call explosive  cyclogenesis a sudden drop in pressure  generating very high winds by around  lunchtime here on the isle of wight  eunice set a new provisional wind speed  record for england a gust of 122 miles  an hour  was this and other extreme gusts caused  by a so-called sting jet phenomenon  where rapidly cooling air further  accelerates winds that was key to  intensifying storms like the great storm  of 1987. there are features in today's  satellite images like these fingers of  cloud that meteorologists think could  indicate that might have occurred but  it'll take more analysis to be sure  however meteorologists say the winds  were strong enough to justify the  warnings given  even three days ago before the storm  even started to form we knew there was  going to be a strong storm approaching  the uk  red warnings were issued  you know this flooding hasn't come to  pass but there's certainly been some  damaging  and very dangerous winds  and if it hadn't been forecast or if  people hadn't taken the warning  seriously i think you know there's every  expectation that you know  the danger to people would have been a  lot worse the first of two red risk to  life warnings for eunice was for the  south west thankfully the timings of  high tide high winds and low pressure  didn't coincide to cause major flooding  there was a storm surge look at the tide  gauge data for newport in south wales  predicted tides in red recorded sea  level in blue nearly two meters higher  but not high enough to over top sea  walls but the winds were severe enough  eunice still a damaging and deadly storm  as she moved into the second red zone of  london and the south east and caused  further disruption as she made her way  north  now eunice is an intense storm and much  has been said about whether climate  change is a contributor to that but  climate scientists say there's no  evidence so far winds have become  stronger or storms more frequent as the  uk has warmed but storm surges and  floods from heavy rain are predicted to  worsen with climate change eunice spared  us the worst of those but that doesn't  mean we'll be so lucky next time
 

